Subject: Economic systems

> Yes ... and no.  I forgot to include a paragraph or two.  An
> interesting thing about the development of a market economy is that it
> seemed to go in phases.  The first market transactions seem to be
> things of significant value, long-distance trading, and the like.

I'd expect merchants to have pioneered formal contract instruments.  That's
consistent with the evidence.  That's different from pioneering *the
market* (that is, allocation of resources by supply and demand using property
rights and negotiation between equal actors as Schelling points).

You seem to be assuming that the carriage trade invented contract instruments
before markets were widespread.  I think it's far more likely that these tools
evolved in a context of low-level, localized market activity that was already
well established by the dawn of written history.

Support for this comes not just from economics (which tells us that it's
the most efficient way to do things, so a culture adopting it is likely
to outcompete those who don't), but also from the new discipline of evolutinary
psychology --- the study of how human evolutionary history is reflected in
our cognitive skills and biases.

One of the clearest lessons of evolutionary psych. to date is that human beings
have a fair amount of semi-instinctual wiring for spotting cheaters and
contract breakers.  How do we know this?  Here'a one way: if you pose the same
negotiating problem in terms of (a) abstract variables, and (b) human actors,
human skill at spotting hidden gotchas is measurably, dramatically greater
for version (b).   There are said to be lots of others, but I haven't read
any of the actual research yet.

Humans are borne to haggle.  They're not just culturally trained into market
negotiation, they're *genetically adapted for it*.  Nature doesn't do these
things quickly, so negotiation must have been selectively important fairly
far back into human and hominid prehistory.

> Also, many of these transactions seem to not have the crucial element
> of well-defined prices.  (According to the law codes I've read,)
> prices seemed to be set by custom or law in a *lot* of transactions,

There are command elements in our economy, too.  Future archeologists
would be likely to see those way overrepresented, too.  After all,
what are they going to like digging in?  The noble ruins of DeeCee
or a corner supermarket?

> You also have to be careful interpreting documents.  A promise to
> deliver 6 rams and 6 ewes in six months doesn't necessarily mean that
> they've been bought.  They could be the inventory of a flock that a
> shepherd has been hired to guard, with the wage being set at the
> increase over the original herd.

What about "This Bill Past Due?"  I wasn't joking.  There are a lot of
those.  You can explain away any individual cases, but the trend in the
mass of documents is overwhelming.

> Similarly, long-range trade can be accomplished via gift-giving exchanges.

Nuts.  There are cultures that give trade the *form* of gift exchanges,
but some of them produce the sharpest hagglers on Earh.
