From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: thinking isn't what you think it is

On Sun, 11 Apr 1993, D Anton Sherwood wrote:

> > . . . . I
> > know that I have sensory evidence that my mental functions are not
> > unitary, but I still perceive my consciousness to be unitary:
> > [describes incident]
> 
> I've had a similar experience recently.  While under the influence of hemp
> smoke, I can't consciously detect some of my faculties -- my time-sense goes
> funny, and I can't tell whether I am speaking coherently for more than a
> (brief) sentence at a time.  But (on the occasion I have in mind) several
> people did listen to me at length, and tell me later that they enjoyed it.
> So some part of what I would normally call my conscious mind retained an
> ability that another part could not detect.  "Awareness" itself may be a
> small and unimportant function (that should start some flames).

I can top this one.  (But all to good purpose; if you just want the purpose
without the story, skip to the next paragraph).  20 years ago I was in
the Peace Corps in Thailand.  We had our training, including language
training, in Thailand.  I was the star language learner, and thus often
called on to demonstrate my prowess to impress locals or laggard fellow
students.  (No, I'm not going to start telling y'all about my GRE scores).
One evening we were all supposed to go to a movie, to increase our exposure
to Thai language and culture.  Well, a few of us had been sampling some
of the local herbal product before we went off to the movie.  We got there,
and there was the head of our language program (an older and very proper
woman from the Ministry of Education), standing around with some local
dignitaries.  Seeing me, she immediately called me over to strut my stuff.
By all external evidence, I proceeded to successfully carry on a conversation
with her in Thai (which that point we had been studying for about 3 weeks)
for 10 or 15 minutes.  But I have, and more to the point had then, no
memory whatever of what I might have said, or what she said, or what we
were talking about, or anything.  (My fellow stoners were sure impressed
as hell, though.  So was I, for that matter).
	But in fact you don't need false drugs, or medical emergencies,
to experience this phenomenon.  Dennett (_Consciousness Explained_)
talks about the similar phenomenon of, for example, driving, while your
thoughts wander, and suddenly realizing that you have no memory at all of 
your driving activities for the last five minutes.
     All of these have in common that you *must* have been in some very
real sense "paying attention" during what in retrospect looks like a fugue.
Your friends would have reacted if you were incoherent.  Ajaan Suraya would
have had fourteen varieties of conniptions if she had suspected for a 
moment that I was under the influence.  If you weren't concentrating on
your driving with some attentive and functioning part of your mind, the
universe would have reminded you in some dramatic fashion of what you
were supposed to be doing.
     Dennett proposes (at great and interesting length) a way of thinking
about what's going on here.  Roughly, what you experience as "consciousness"
is that part what's going on in your brain that, so to speak, spawns further
processes.  Occasionally events show you a situation where something that
you would expect to have left a memory trace hasn't, but actually that's
going on all the time--only most of the time it's only the stuff that's
*made* conscious that you have any evidence for, and thence comes the
illusion of unitary linear consciousness.  Probably the fabled short-term
memory effects of cannabis intoxication make a palpable mismatch between
expectation and actual recorded consciousness more likely, but the 
phenomenon is really quite normal.

> Similarly, while drugged I sometimes seem to have poor balance, but as far
> as I can tell (from admittedly suspect memory) I'm no clumsier than usual.

No, the clumsiness is real.  Trust me.


Scott DeLancey



