X-Reposting-Policy: Redistribute freely

I got hold of some of the papers Robin and Mike recommended.  This fellow
Matt Visser writes about how he is working on ways to create the
wormholes.  One of his designs is as Mike said, a cube-shaped frame with
negative mass-energy along the edges.  You need -1.52*10^43 Joules per
meter.  Visser writes:

"Needless to say, energies and tensions of this magnitude (let alone
_sign_) are well beyond current technological capabilities."

He concludes:  "[T]he required presence of exotic matter, while it is
not a cause for panic, is certainly a cause for concern.  I have
attempted to obtain the required exotic stress energy by considering
the Casimir energy associated with oscillations of a classical string.
So far I have been unable to do so."

Actually, I have an idea.  It turns out that whether the polyhedron face
is convex (pointing out) or concave (pointing in) determines whether the
needed stress-energy is negative or positive.  Since polyhedrons are convex
on average, the needed energy must be negative.

My idea is to construct a polyhedron that is concave, by the simple
expedient of locating yourself inside of it.  This is concave at each edge,
so now all you need is a large amount of (positive) energy there.

I'm being a little facetious, but this reminds me of one of my favorite
high-IQ stories: "Star Bright", which I read in the anthology "Tomorrow's
Children" many years ago.  It's about a really, really bright little girl
named Star.  Among the things she discovers is that if you mentally wrap
a Klein bottle (the 3-dimensional analog of a Mobius strip) around yourself
you can teleport anywhere in spacetime.

Maybe this is an advanced version of Visser's idea.

