>sets forth about as good a set of rules as *humans* can generate. 

I bet even *humans* could do better, though I don't have the solution at my
fingertips.

>                                                                   This by
>no means indicates that I am satisfied, so I'm not surprised that you
>aren't either.  It's an imperfect system, but then humans are rather far
>from perfect themselves.  Is there a better way to measure informed consent
>than by having people attest that they knowingly agree?  [. . .]
>T.O. Morrow                  

If UCC is so imperfect, what does that say about refusing to enforce an
unconscionable contract? Perhaps that is also imperfect but the best
alternative, given that UCC is so weak.

Let me summarize my position, and see if/where we still disagree:

1) Common law has been gutted by the State.

2) A revitalized common law would be an acceptable form of PPL. Agents make
law by contracting around precedents, rulings, legislation, etc. This
de-monopolizes the making of law, putting it in the hands of the persons
most impacted by it.

3) 2 makes the system potentially circular. This is okay since society
itself is potentially (though rarely in practice) circular or
contradictory. A society that enforces slave contracts one year may change
the next, and could imaginably change again. The legal system needs to be
able to reflect the learning and experimenting of those under its
jurisdiction.

4) Unconscionable contracts should not be enforced until there is a robust
mechanism for determining informed consent (other than sending every adult
to law school). There are still problems with this (particularly, who
decides what is or isn't unconscionable), but it seems reasonable in the
current context.

5) Property rights are not platonic ideals. They are the result of a
process of dispute, agreement, and learning. They have changed over
history, and will likely continue to change. This is true of both narrow
property rights (ownership of objects) and of property rights viewed
broadly as that to which persons make claims (information, obligations,
rights, restrictions on behavior, lack of restriction, sexual harrassmant,
etc.).

6) It's misleading to call a society that restricts employment contracts
less free than one that doesn't. Practical differences will certainly
result, but not differences of freedom. If we're talking about a PPL
utopia, then either situation is changeable by contract or by dispute
resolution into the other, and the restriction is a cost rather than a
proscription. If we're talking about a statist world like the one we
inhabit, whatever the State does might (by accident) be identical to what
would have resulted from free choice. For the State to enforce employment
contracts is as intrusive as it is for it to disallow their enforcement.
The State could construct a market utopia, a sort of super-Chile, where the
right of contract was absolute and pristine. But if people are not allowed
to experiment, to disagree, to restrict contracts if they choose, they
would not be free. Is this my criterion of freedom, at last? Let me think
on it.

7) The difference between employment contracts and murder contracts lies
entirely in our attitudes toward them. That is still an important, even
crucial, difference. But that same difference could separate conscionable
from unconscionable contracts. Our legal system should express what we are,
not what we could imagine ourselves to be.
