Date: Tue, 13 Apr 1993 08:38:32 +0000
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: LAW: Employment contracts

>Dave
>>Me

>>The Uniform Commercial Code has widely accepted criteria for establishing
>>informed consent, [. . .]

>Is that the bit at the end of the contract? . . . That's it? That's all?

No.  The UCC is not at any particular part of the contract.  It is rather a
set of rules that set up default terms (eg, implied warrants of fitness),
contractual forms (eg, waivers of warrants must appear in large, bold
print), and interpretive strategies (eg, a contract should be read so as to
make it consistent throughout).  Interestingly, the UCC incorporates the
law merchant, one of the world's oldest and most widely used polycentric
legal systems.

>I bet even *humans* could do better [than the UCC], though I don't have the >solution at my fingertips.

Ah, for an ideas future market . . . 8-)  

Take note that humans worldwide have long had powerful incentives to
maximize the efficiency of contract law.  This doesn't mean they've
succeeded, of course, but it does indicate that basic improvements are
unlikely.

>If UCC is so imperfect, what does that say about refusing to enforce an
>unconscionable contract? 

Actually, the UCC does have a provision that takes into account that courts
may find contracts unconscionable (section 2-302).  And it regards as
unenforceable contracts that fail to meet certain formats (eg, section
2-201(1) holds (with some exceptions) that a sale of goods priced at >$500
is not enforceable unless there is a signed piece of writing).  The UCC
does not, however, say anything about the conscionability of the substance
of contracts.  It doesn't say, for example, that you cannot enforce a
contract to sell sexual favors. 

That the UCC is imperfect says nothing about the fact that it does not
reach the substance of unconscionable contracts.  Any policy that refuses
to enforce contracts for certain goods or service is also going to be
imperfect.  I argue that it will be more imperfect, at least with regard to
consensual exchanges.

>Let me summarize my position, and see if/where we still disagree:
>
>1) Common law has been gutted by the State.

Too strong a claim.  I'll grant that it is true in the U.S. with regard to
many areas of law, however.

>2) A revitalized common law would be an acceptable form of PPL. Agents make
>law by contracting around precedents, rulings, legislation, etc. 

No, I think this misunderstands the relation between contracts and
commonlaw.  PPL would arise if common law courts agreed to enforce all
manner of contracts that meet the standards of the UCC (or of some
equivalently good system of ensuring that the parties to a contract consent
to its terms).  Agents are thus not contracting around the law generally,
for the law protects their contracts.  Rather, they would be contracting
around particular default rules of, say, tort or employment law.

>6) It's misleading to call a society that restricts employment contracts
>less free than one that doesn't. 

I think I have already put forward argument that refute this claim.

>7) The difference between employment contracts and murder contracts lies
>entirely in our attitudes toward them.

This is too vague to permit intelligent commentary.  The *only* difference?
 Whose attitudes?  Can you define "attitude" so that it is not
tautologically true that only attitudes ever count? (Eg, since we only
perceive the external world through a filter of attitudes, how can we ever
get around them?)


