Date: Tue, 13 Apr 1993 17:14:07 +0200 (MET DST)
From: ggoebel@sun1.ruf.uni-freiburg.de (Garrett Goebel)
Subject: Phonics

Claim:  Teaching phonetics does not correlate with higher "IQ", reading
ability, or intelligence.

Duncan Frissell writes:
} See Spot run.  Run Spot run.  See Jane run.  Run Jane run.  See Jane run 
} after Spot.  Run Spot run.

S-EE sp-O-t r-U-n?

One of the big problems with using phonetics in the English language
is that if you look at the way things are spelled... and the way they
are pronounced... the correlation isn't too good.  Italian is much 
better correlating sounds and letter... but then, we don't all speak
italian...

I mean, try pronouncing "phonetics".  P-hOn-EE-t-eye-cs
Or try pronouncing "brother"... brOthEr.
Try reading any sentence I or you write... and pronounce the
letters like you were taught in you "ABC's".

English as written is much more representative of English
as it was spoken at the end of the middle ages.  If you really 
want phonetics to work, you're going to have to explain how
letters and letter combinations developed into the sounds as
we now use them.  You'd have to teach that 6 year old the 
history of the English language... indogermanic... and maybe
even a little indoeuropean.  All of which would be a major pain,
and more or less a waste of time.  A waste because kids learn
phonetics anyways.

I myself am an non-phonetic child.  It was taken out of the 
school system at the time when I was entering it... and my 
reading ability and vocabulary have had very little to do with
phonetics.  Hell, my vocabulary is better, and was better than
most of the kids one or two grades higher (who had had phonetics).
I read all the "young adult" books in elementary school, and by
junior high I was reading "adult" literature such as Heinlein,
Asimov, Clavell's _Noble_House_ and _Shogun_, Durrant's histories
...basically about everything you'd expect a halfway intelligent 
adult to read.

Sounding a word out isn't something that has to be taught in school.
I learned how to do it quite well on my own.  Even today, I still
"mispronounce" many words... I mispronounce them, because I sound
them out the way they are written not the way they are spoken.  I 
was never "taught" to sound words out... and I never needed to be 
"taught".  I taught myself.

I agree much more with the previous threads which explain the 
difference in reading ability and intelligence along the lines
of teaching philosophies... and individual motivation.  But then,
I'm biased, I was not raised under phonetics.  Most of you are
biased... because you were.

                           Garrett


