Subject: PHONICS & Nihonjin

edgar@spectrx.saigon.com (Edgar W. Swank) says:
>It was interesting to read Duncan Frissell's post of the article by
>Regna Lee Wood, laying the blame for American illiteracy to the switch
>from phonics to "see and say".
>
>Although I expect there's a lot of truth to that, I'd be interested in
>Duncan's comments (or Regna's or Flesch's, if available) in the high
>literacy rates in Japan (for example) which does not even -have- a
>phonetic alphabet. (or perhaps has one but doesn't use it much).

There has been recent post on the non-phonetic Japanese language being
taught phonetically:

from "W. Scott Meeks" <meeks@osf.org>:
>As I understand it, Japanese children are first taught hiragana, and that
>early children's books are written entirely in hiragana.  Because the
>mapping from hiragana to sounds is so direct (unlike the mapping from the
>English alphabet to English sounds) there is no reason to learn to read
>hiragana in any way other than analogously to phonics.  
>
>Next, Japanese children start learning individual Kanji by having their
>sounds explained and by having the sound written in furigana next to or on
>top of the Kanji.

What I'm getting from this whole debate is that neither method is the
optimum solution. The best solution is, as usual, to combine the two
methods (Hegelian Dialectic anyone?). Start with Phonics to get the vocab,
and then switch to see-and-say to get the spelling. If you'll note, the
above quote from Scott seems to indicate that is precisely what the
Japanese do.

(Nihonjin, btw, is the Japanese name for their people.)

